IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `coroners-dates`
IMPORT `coroners-family`

-- Coroners Act 1997 (ACT) -- worked cases, from the seat of a member of the deceased's immediate family.
--
-- Every assertion is a claim about the ENCODING on stated facts, not about the law. Expected values were written by hand
-- from the deposited text (A1997-57, R51), never copied from the encoding's output. No scenario life raised this Act;
-- the facts are the encoder's, HYPOTHETICAL throughout. Every opinion, belief or satisfaction of an official is given as
-- a fact.
--
-- A breached DEONTIC cannot be compared with EQUALS on the stable l4, so the regulative rule is asserted through
-- CONSIDER ... WHEN FULFILLED, and one breach is also #TRACEd with an "-- expect:" line.

GIVEN d IS A NUMBER, m IS A NUMBER, y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`on` d m y MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY d m y

§ `Dictionary -- member of the immediate family`

#ASSERT `Am I a member of the immediate family question` `parent` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: I was the deceased's domestic partner, parent, step-parent, grandparent, child, brother or sister, guardian or ward (dictionary, member of the immediate family, paragraph (a))`
#ASSERT `Am I a member of the immediate family question` `brother or sister` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: I was the deceased's domestic partner, parent, step-parent, grandparent, child, brother or sister, guardian or ward (dictionary, member of the immediate family, paragraph (a))`
#ASSERT `Am I a member of the immediate family question` `ward` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: I was the deceased's domestic partner, parent, step-parent, grandparent, child, brother or sister, guardian or ward (dictionary, member of the immediate family, paragraph (a))`
-- An aunt of an Aboriginal person who, under the community's customs, had responsibility for their welfare.
#ASSERT `Am I a member of the immediate family question` `another relative, or a friend` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the deceased was an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person and under the traditions and customs of their community I had the responsibility for, or an interest in, their welfare (dictionary, paragraph (b))`
#ASSERT `Am I a member of the immediate family question` `another relative, or a friend` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: the definition of member of the immediate family does not include me`
-- Paragraph (b) needs the deceased to have been Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander.
#ASSERT `Am I a member of the immediate family question` `another relative, or a friend` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: the definition of member of the immediate family does not include me`

§ `ss 3BB, 3C -- death in care, death in custody`

#ASSERT `Was it a death in care or custody question` `being taken into or detained in custody, or subject to an order, under the Mental Health Act 2015` `no one (the question does not arise)` EQUALS `a death in care (s 3BB)`
#ASSERT `Was it a death in care or custody question` `being taken into or detained in custody, or subject to an order, under the Crimes Act 1900 section 309 (assessment whether emergency detention is required)` `a police officer` EQUALS `a death in care (s 3BB)`
#ASSERT `Was it a death in care or custody question` `at a correction centre, lockup or detention place` `no one (the question does not arise)` EQUALS `a death in custody (s 3C)`
#ASSERT `Was it a death in care or custody question` `performing community service work under a good behaviour order (Crimes (Sentencing) Act 2005)` `no one (the question does not arise)` EQUALS `a death in custody (s 3C)`
#ASSERT `Was it a death in care or custody question` `subject to an accommodation order for a young offender (Crimes (Sentencing) Act 2005 section 133Z)` `no one (the question does not arise)` EQUALS `a death in custody (s 3C)`
#ASSERT `Was it a death in care or custody question` `subject to an interstate transfer arrangement, or in transit custody, under the Children and Young People Act 2008` `no one (the question does not arise)` EQUALS `a death in custody (s 3C)`
#ASSERT `Was it a death in care or custody question` `at a licensed place under the Intoxicated People (Care and Protection) Act 1994` `no one (the question does not arise)` EQUALS `a death in custody (s 3C)`
#ASSERT `Was it a death in care or custody question` `in, being taken into, or after being taken into, someone's custody` `a police officer` EQUALS `a death in custody (s 3C)`
#ASSERT `Was it a death in care or custody question` `in, being taken into, or after being taken into, someone's custody` `the sheriff, a deputy sheriff, or a person appointed to assist the sheriff` EQUALS `a death in custody (s 3C)`
#ASSERT `Was it a death in care or custody question` `in, being taken into, or after being taken into, someone's custody` `a carer under the Intoxicated People (Care and Protection) Act 1994` EQUALS `a death in custody (s 3C)`
-- A security guard is not a custodial officer (s 3D).
#ASSERT `Was it a death in care or custody question` `in, being taken into, or after being taken into, someone's custody` `someone else (for example a security guard or a hospital)` EQUALS `neither a death in care nor a death in custody (ss 3BB, 3C)`
#ASSERT `Was it a death in care or custody question` `escaping, or attempting to escape, from someone's custody` `a corrections officer` EQUALS `a death in custody (s 3C)`
-- s 3C(1)(g) excludes escape from an Intoxicated People Act carer (fork register).
#ASSERT `Was it a death in care or custody question` `escaping, or attempting to escape, from someone's custody` `a carer under the Intoxicated People (Care and Protection) Act 1994` EQUALS `neither a death in care nor a death in custody (ss 3BB, 3C)`
#ASSERT `Was it a death in care or custody question` `none of these` `no one (the question does not arise)` EQUALS `neither a death in care nor a death in custody (ss 3BB, 3C)`
#ASSERT `Was it a death in care or custody question` `in, being taken into, or after being taken into, someone's custody` `no one (the question does not arise)` EQUALS `neither a death in care nor a death in custody (ss 3BB, 3C)`

§ `s 13 -- must there be an inquest`

-- Arguments: in the ACT, resident, (a), (b), (c), (d), (e), (f), (g), (h), where, whose custody, VAD.
-- Nothing in s 13(1) applies.
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `none of these` `no one (the question does not arise)` FALSE EQUALS `not covered: s 13 does not require an inquest on these facts; its list is not closed, and s 12 keeps the coroner's other jurisdiction`
-- Each paragraph (a) to (h) alone.
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `none of these` `no one (the question does not arise)` FALSE EQUALS `yes: a coroner must hold an inquest into the manner and cause of death (s 13(1))`
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `none of these` `no one (the question does not arise)` FALSE EQUALS `yes: a coroner must hold an inquest into the manner and cause of death (s 13(1))`
-- A death after surgery, appearing partly attributable to it: the medical-negligence case.
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `none of these` `no one (the question does not arise)` FALSE EQUALS `yes: a coroner must hold an inquest into the manner and cause of death (s 13(1))`
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `none of these` `no one (the question does not arise)` FALSE EQUALS `yes: a coroner must hold an inquest into the manner and cause of death (s 13(1))`
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE `none of these` `no one (the question does not arise)` FALSE EQUALS `yes: a coroner must hold an inquest into the manner and cause of death (s 13(1))`
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE `none of these` `no one (the question does not arise)` FALSE EQUALS `yes: a coroner must hold an inquest into the manner and cause of death (s 13(1))`
-- A worker killed in a workplace accident.
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE `none of these` `no one (the question does not arise)` FALSE EQUALS `yes: a coroner must hold an inquest into the manner and cause of death (s 13(1))`
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE `none of these` `no one (the question does not arise)` FALSE EQUALS `yes: a coroner must hold an inquest into the manner and cause of death (s 13(1))`
-- (i): a death in a lockup; a death in care under the Mental Health Act with an accident as well.
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `at a correction centre, lockup or detention place` `no one (the question does not arise)` FALSE EQUALS `yes: a coroner must hold an inquest, and the death is a death in care or custody, so Part 6 also applies (s 13(1)(i))`
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE `being taken into or detained in custody, or subject to an order, under the Mental Health Act 2015` `no one (the question does not arise)` FALSE EQUALS `yes: a coroner must hold an inquest, and the death is a death in care or custody, so Part 6 also applies (s 13(1)(i))`
-- s 13(2): a voluntary assisted death of a person in care, with nothing else.
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `being taken into or detained in custody, or subject to an order, under the Mental Health Act 2015` `no one (the question does not arise)` TRUE EQUALS `no: s 13(1)(i) does not apply to a person who self-administered, or was administered, an approved substance under the Voluntary Assisted Dying Act 2024, and no other paragraph of s 13(1) applies (s 13(2))`
-- s 13(2) takes out (i) only: with (e) as well, an inquest is still required.
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE `being taken into or detained in custody, or subject to an order, under the Mental Health Act 2015` `no one (the question does not arise)` TRUE EQUALS `yes: a coroner must hold an inquest into the manner and cause of death (s 13(1))`
-- A voluntary assisted death not in care or custody, nothing else.
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `none of these` `no one (the question does not arise)` TRUE EQUALS `not covered: s 13 does not require an inquest on these facts; its list is not closed, and s 12 keeps the coroner's other jurisdiction`
-- s 13(3): an ACT resident killed in an accident interstate; a non-resident; a resident with nothing in s 13(1).
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE `none of these` `no one (the question does not arise)` FALSE EQUALS `a coroner may hold an inquest but is not required to: the death happened outside the ACT to a person ordinarily resident in the ACT, in circumstances s 13(1) lists (s 13(3))`
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE `none of these` `no one (the question does not arise)` FALSE EQUALS `no: the death happened outside the ACT to a person not ordinarily resident in the ACT, so s 13(3) gives no jurisdiction`
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `none of these` `no one (the question does not arise)` FALSE EQUALS `not covered: s 13 does not require an inquest on these facts; its list is not closed, and s 12 keeps the coroner's other jurisdiction`
#ASSERT `Must there be an inquest question` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `in, being taken into, or after being taken into, someone's custody` `a police officer` FALSE EQUALS `a coroner may hold an inquest but is not required to: the death happened outside the ACT to a person ordinarily resident in the ACT, in circumstances s 13(1) lists (s 13(3))`

§ `s 68A -- told of the inquest`

#ASSERT `Will the family be told of the inquest question` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the coroner must tell a member of the immediate family that an inquest will be held; the coroner chooses who (one able to pass the information on), so it may not be me (s 68A(1), (4))`
#ASSERT `Will the family be told of the inquest question` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: the coroner is satisfied on reasonable grounds that no member of the immediate family wishes to be told, or that it is impracticable to tell any of them (s 68A(3)(a))`
#ASSERT `Will the family be told of the inquest question` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: the coroner is satisfied on reasonable grounds that no member of the immediate family wishes to be told, or that it is impracticable to tell any of them (s 68A(3)(a))`
#ASSERT `Will the family be told of the inquest question` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 68A applies only where a coroner is required to hold an inquest`

§ `s 15 -- release of the body`

#ASSERT `Can the body be released question` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the coroner has given a certificate authorising the release of the body (s 15(3))`
#ASSERT `Can the body be released question` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not yet, but the coroner may now give a release certificate, being satisfied there is no reason the body should not be buried, cremated or taken out of the ACT for that (s 15(3)); until then the coroner controls the body (s 15(2))`
#ASSERT `Can the body be released question` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not yet: the coroner controls the body until a release certificate is given, and may give one only when satisfied there is no reason the body should not be buried, cremated or taken out of the ACT for that (s 15(2), (3))`
#ASSERT `Can the body be released question` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 15 does not give the coroner control of the body: it applies only where an inquest is required and the body is in the ACT (s 15(1))`
#ASSERT `Can the body be released question` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 15 does not give the coroner control of the body: it applies only where an inquest is required and the body is in the ACT (s 15(1))`

§ `s 20 -- no post-mortem`

#ASSERT `Can I ask that there be no post-mortem question` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `possibly: on my request the coroner may dispense with the post-mortem examination, being satisfied that the manner and cause of death are sufficiently disclosed (s 20(2)); it is a discretion`
#ASSERT `Can I ask that there be no post-mortem question` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: the coroner may dispense with the post-mortem examination only if satisfied that the manner and cause of death are sufficiently disclosed (s 20)`
#ASSERT `Can I ask that there be no post-mortem question` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `s 20(2) gives the request only to a member of the immediate family or their representative; the coroner may still dispense with the examination on the information before it (s 20(1))`

§ `ss 23, 70 -- viewing, inspecting, attending`

-- Arguments: what, family member, care or custody, not in interests of justice, not in public interest.
#ASSERT `Must the coroner allow my request question` `to view the body` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: for a death in care or custody the coroner must authorise it (s 70(1))`
#ASSERT `Must the coroner allow my request question` `for the same or another doctor to conduct a further post-mortem examination` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: for a death in care or custody the coroner must authorise it (s 70(1))`
#ASSERT `Must the coroner allow my request question` `to inspect the scene of the death` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no, but the coroner believes on reasonable grounds it would not be in the interests of justice, and must give me written notice of the decision and the reasons (s 70(1), (2))`
#ASSERT `Must the coroner allow my request question` `to inspect the scene of an event that may have resulted in the death` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: for a death in care or custody, s 23 does not apply and s 70 does not list an inspection of the scene of another event (ss 23(1), 70(1))`
#ASSERT `Must the coroner allow my request question` `to inspect the scene of an event that may have resulted in the death` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `possibly: the coroner may authorise it; it is a discretion (s 23(1))`
#ASSERT `Must the coroner allow my request question` `to be present at the post-mortem examination` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `possibly: the coroner may authorise it; it is a discretion (s 23(1))`
#ASSERT `Must the coroner allow my request question` `to view the body` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `possibly, but if the coroner refuses because it would not be in the public interest or the interests of justice, I must be given written notice with an explanation and a statement that I may apply to the Chief Coroner to have the decision reconsidered (s 23(2), (3), s 24)`
#ASSERT `Must the coroner allow my request question` `to view the body` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `possibly, but if the coroner refuses because it would not be in the public interest or the interests of justice, I must be given written notice with an explanation and a statement that I may apply to the Chief Coroner to have the decision reconsidered (s 23(2), (3), s 24)`
-- For a death in care or custody, the public interest is not a ground of refusal (s 70(1) names only the interests of justice).
#ASSERT `Must the coroner allow my request question` `to view the body` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: for a death in care or custody the coroner must authorise it (s 70(1))`
#ASSERT `Must the coroner allow my request question` `to view the body` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: only a member of the immediate family or their representative can make the request (ss 23(1), 70(1))`

§ `s 24 -- reconsideration of a refusal`

#ASSERT `Can I have a refusal reconsidered question` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the Chief Coroner must ask the coroner to reconsider, and must tell me of any comments the coroner makes in response (s 24(1), (3))`
#ASSERT `Can I have a refusal reconsidered question` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not yet: the application to the Chief Coroner must be in writing and set out the grounds I rely on (s 24(1)(b), (2))`
#ASSERT `Can I have a refusal reconsidered question` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: the Chief Coroner must ask for reconsideration only if satisfied that I have a sufficient interest in the decision (s 24(1))`
#ASSERT `Can I have a refusal reconsidered question` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: s 24 applies only to a refusal under s 23(1), which does not cover a death in care or custody (s 70(2) gives written reasons instead)`

§ `s 34A -- must there be a hearing`

#ASSERT `Must there be a hearing question` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the coroner must not dispense with a hearing, having reasonable grounds to believe the death is a death in care or custody (s 34A(2))`
#ASSERT `Must there be a hearing question` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `not necessarily: the coroner may decide not to hold a hearing; if so, the Chief Coroner and a member of the immediate family must get written notice of the decision and its grounds (s 34A(1), (3)), and I may apply for a hearing or for the decision to be reconsidered (s 64)`
#ASSERT `Must there be a hearing question` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the coroner cannot decide against a hearing under s 34A unless satisfied both that the manner and cause of death are sufficiently disclosed and that a hearing is unnecessary (s 34A(1))`
#ASSERT `Must there be a hearing question` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `the coroner cannot decide against a hearing under s 34A unless satisfied both that the manner and cause of death are sufficiently disclosed and that a hearing is unnecessary (s 34A(1))`

§ `ss 37, 69 -- hearing without the family being told`

-- Arguments: care or custody, notified, reasonable efforts, Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander, legal service notified, public interest.
#ASSERT `Can the hearing go ahead without the family being told question` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the family has been notified as the Act requires (s 69(1))`
#ASSERT `Can the hearing go ahead without the family being told question` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the family has been notified as the Act requires (s 69(1))`
#ASSERT `Can the hearing go ahead without the family being told question` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the family has been notified as the Act requires (s 69(1))`
-- An Aboriginal man dies in custody; his mother is told, but the local Aboriginal legal service is not.
#ASSERT `Can the hearing go ahead without the family being told question` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the coroner must not hold the hearing until satisfied a member of the immediate family has been notified, or reasonable efforts to notify one have failed, and, for an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person, the local Aboriginal legal service has been notified (s 69(1))`
#ASSERT `Can the hearing go ahead without the family being told question` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the coroner must not hold the hearing until satisfied a member of the immediate family has been notified, or reasonable efforts to notify one have failed, and, for an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person, the local Aboriginal legal service has been notified (s 69(1))`
#ASSERT `Can the hearing go ahead without the family being told question` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes, but only because the coroner believes on reasonable grounds that it is in the public interest or the interests of justice (s 69(2))`
#ASSERT `Can the hearing go ahead without the family being told question` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the coroner has notified a member of the immediate family, or taken reasonable steps to (s 37(1))`
-- For a death not in care or custody, s 37 does not ask for the Aboriginal legal service to be notified.
#ASSERT `Can the hearing go ahead without the family being told question` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the coroner has notified a member of the immediate family, or taken reasonable steps to (s 37(1))`
#ASSERT `Can the hearing go ahead without the family being told question` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes, the coroner may go ahead, believing on reasonable grounds that it is in the public interest or the interests of justice (s 37(2))`
#ASSERT `Can the hearing go ahead without the family being told question` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not yet: before the hearing the coroner must take reasonable steps, at the earliest opportunity, to notify a member of the immediate family of its time and place (s 37(1))`

§ `s 38 -- public notice 28 days before the hearing`

-- A hearing on 10 March 2027: 28 days before is 10 February 2027 (February 2027 has 28 days).
#ASSERT `Last day for public notice of the hearing question` (`on` 10 3 2027) EQUALS `on` 10 2 2027
-- A hearing on 28 March 2028: 28 days before is 29 February 2028 (a leap year).
#ASSERT `Last day for public notice of the hearing question` (`on` 28 3 2028) EQUALS `on` 29 2 2028
-- Notice on the last day; a day late; a day late where it was not practicable; well ahead.
#ASSERT `Was public notice in time question` (`on` 10 3 2027) (`on` 10 2 2027) TRUE EQUALS `in time: public notice was given at least 28 days before the hearing (s 38)`
#ASSERT `Was public notice in time question` (`on` 10 3 2027) (`on` 11 2 2027) TRUE EQUALS `late: public notice was given less than 28 days before the hearing, and it was practicable to give it in time (s 38)`
#ASSERT `Was public notice in time question` (`on` 10 3 2027) (`on` 11 2 2027) FALSE EQUALS `s 38 asks for 28 days' notice only where practicable; the coroner considered it was not practicable`
#ASSERT `Was public notice in time question` (`on` 10 3 2027) (`on` 4 1 2027) TRUE EQUALS `in time: public notice was given at least 28 days before the hearing (s 38)`

§ `ss 42, 49, 57A`

#ASSERT `Can I take part in the hearing question` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the coroner has given me leave to appear in person or by a lawyer and to examine and cross-examine witnesses (s 42)`
#ASSERT `Can I take part in the hearing question` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `I may ask for leave: the coroner may let me appear in person or by a lawyer and examine and cross-examine witnesses (s 42); it is a discretion`
#ASSERT `Can I take part in the hearing question` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `I may ask for leave: the coroner may let me appear in person or by a lawyer and examine and cross-examine witnesses (s 42); it is a discretion`
#ASSERT `Can I take part in the hearing question` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: s 42 leave is for a person summoned to give evidence or whom the coroner considers to have a sufficient interest; the hearing itself is in public unless the coroner orders otherwise (s 40)`

#ASSERT `Can I get a copy of the record question` TRUE EQUALS `yes: having satisfied the registrar or a coroner that I have good reason, I am entitled to a copy of the record (s 49(2)(c))`
#ASSERT `Can I get a copy of the record question` FALSE EQUALS `no: I am entitled to a copy of the record only if I satisfy the registrar or a coroner that I have good reason (s 49(2)(c))`

#ASSERT `Can a mistake in the findings be corrected question` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes, I may ask: the coroner may amend the finding or report to correct a mistake, error or omission, on my request as a person with sufficient interest (s 57A); it is a discretion`
#ASSERT `Can a mistake in the findings be corrected question` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `s 57A lets a person with sufficient interest ask; the coroner may still correct it on his or her own initiative (s 57A(2)(a))`
#ASSERT `Can a mistake in the findings be corrected question` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: s 57A is for correcting a mistake, error or omission, not for changing a finding I disagree with (see ss 64, 68, 93)`

§ `ss 52, 54, 74, 75, 76 -- the findings`

#ASSERT `What must the findings cover question` FALSE EQUALS `the identity of the deceased, when and where the death happened, and the manner and cause of death, so far as possible; and whether a matter of public safety arises, with comment if it does (s 52(1), (4))`
#ASSERT `What must the findings cover question` TRUE EQUALS `those, and also findings about the quality of care, treatment and supervision of the deceased that the coroner thinks contributed to the cause of death (ss 52, 74)`

-- Arguments: family member, witness who appeared, care or custody, asked.
#ASSERT `Can I get a copy of the findings question` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: for a death in care or custody the coroner must make a copy of the report of the findings available to me without my asking, and later a copy of the agency's response (ss 75(2), 76(4))`
#ASSERT `Can I get a copy of the findings question` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: for a death in care or custody the coroner must make a copy of the report of the findings available to me without my asking, and later a copy of the agency's response (ss 75(2), 76(4))`
#ASSERT `Can I get a copy of the findings question` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: I have asked, and the coroner must make a copy of the findings available to me (s 54(1))`
#ASSERT `Can I get a copy of the findings question` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes, if I ask: on my request the coroner must make a copy of the findings available to me (s 54(1))`
-- A witness at an ordinary inquest gets no copy under s 54.
#ASSERT `Can I get a copy of the findings question` FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not covered: ss 54 and 75 give a copy only to a member of the immediate family or their representative (and, for a death in care or custody, a witness who appeared)`
#ASSERT `Can I get a copy of the findings question` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `not covered: ss 54 and 75 give a copy only to a member of the immediate family or their representative (and, for a death in care or custody, a witness who appeared)`

-- s 76(1): 3 months after receipt. 15 January 2027 -> 15 April 2027; 30 November 2026 -> 28 February 2027 (no 30
-- February); 30 November 2027 -> 29 February 2028 (a leap year).
#ASSERT `Last day for the agency's response question` (`on` 15 1 2027) EQUALS `on` 15 4 2027
#ASSERT `Last day for the agency's response question` (`on` 30 11 2026) EQUALS `on` 28 2 2027
#ASSERT `Last day for the agency's response question` (`on` 30 11 2027) EQUALS `on` 29 2 2028

§ `ss 58, 58A -- when the paused inquest may go on`

-- (a) Notice to the DPP on 10 January 2027: 3 months on is 10 April 2027; a prosecution may be started on or before 11
-- April; if none is, the inquest may go on from 12 April 2027.
#ASSERT `When may the paused inquest go on question` `no prosecution has been started` (`on` 10 1 2027) EQUALS `the inquest may go on from` (`on` 12 4 2027)
-- (a) Notice on 30 November 2026: 3 months on is 28 February 2027, the day after is 1 March, so from 2 March 2027.
#ASSERT `When may the paused inquest go on question` `no prosecution has been started` (`on` 30 11 2026) EQUALS `the inquest may go on from` (`on` 2 3 2027)
-- (b)(i) The DPP's notice of no indictment on 1 March 2027: from 2 March 2027.
#ASSERT `When may the paused inquest go on question` `the DPP has given notice that no indictment will be presented, or that the proceeding has been or will be discontinued` (`on` 1 3 2027) EQUALS `the inquest may go on from` (`on` 2 3 2027)
-- (b)(ii) Discharged on 1 March 2027: 30 days after is 31 March, so from 1 April 2027.
#ASSERT `When may the paused inquest go on question` `the person was not committed for trial (discharged) and was not indicted within 28 days` (`on` 1 3 2027) EQUALS `the inquest may go on from` (`on` 1 4 2027)
-- (b)(iii) The DPP's notice of a final decision on 31 December 2027: "the day after the day" is 1 January 2028, so from 2 January 2028.
#ASSERT `When may the paused inquest go on question` `the person was committed for trial or indicted, and the DPP has given notice that the proceeding has been finally decided` (`on` 31 12 2027) EQUALS `the inquest may go on from` (`on` 2 1 2028)
-- (b)(iv) Found guilty, finally decided 15 February 2028: 30 days after is 16 March 2028 (leap year), so from 17 March 2028.
#ASSERT `When may the paused inquest go on question` `the person was found guilty, and the DPP has given no notice that the proceeding has been finally decided` (`on` 15 2 2028) EQUALS `the inquest may go on from` (`on` 17 3 2028)
#ASSERT `When may the paused inquest go on question` `a prosecution is under way and none of these has happened` (`on` 1 1 2027) EQUALS `not yet: while the prosecution runs, the inquest may only establish the death, the person's identity and the date and place of death (s 58(3)(b), (5))`

§ `s 64 -- asking the Chief Coroner`

-- Arguments: what, in writing, sufficient interest, Chief Coroner's own decision.
#ASSERT `Can I ask for a hearing or a reconsideration question` `a hearing` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `possibly: the Chief Coroner may arrange for a hearing on my written application (s 64(1)); it is a discretion`
#ASSERT `Can I ask for a hearing or a reconsideration question` `reconsideration of a decision to dispense with or to conclude a hearing` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Chief Coroner must ask the coroner to reconsider; the coroner must respond within 14 days of the request, and within 14 days of that response the Chief Coroner must tell me in writing whether a hearing will be held (s 64(2), (5), (7))`
#ASSERT `Can I ask for a hearing or a reconsideration question` `reconsideration of a decision to dispense with or to conclude a hearing` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the decision was the Chief Coroner's own, and the Chief Coroner must reconsider it (s 64(3))`
#ASSERT `Can I ask for a hearing or a reconsideration question` `reconsideration of a decision to dispense with or to conclude a hearing` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the Chief Coroner must act on my application only if satisfied that I have a sufficient interest in the inquest (s 64(2)(b), (3))`
#ASSERT `Can I ask for a hearing or a reconsideration question` `reconsideration of a decision to dispense with or to conclude a hearing` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: the Chief Coroner must act on my application only if satisfied that I have a sufficient interest in the inquest (s 64(2)(b), (3))`
#ASSERT `Can I ask for a hearing or a reconsideration question` `reconsideration of a decision to dispense with or to conclude a hearing` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not yet: my application must be in writing and set out the grounds I rely on (s 64(4))`

-- The regulative rule, in days from the coroner's receipt of the request.
-- The coroner responds on the last day (14); the Chief Coroner gives notice of a hearing on the last day (28).
`response on day 14, hearing notice on day 28` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `s 64(5), (7) and s 90 -- what must happen after my request is passed on` 0 (LIST (EVENT `the coroner who made the decision` `respond to the Chief Coroner's request to reconsider` 14), (EVENT `the Chief Coroner` `give me written notice that a hearing will be conducted` 28))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
#ASSERT `response on day 14, hearing notice on day 28` EQUALS TRUE
-- The coroner responds on day 3; the Chief Coroner says no hearing on day 17; I apply to the Supreme Court on day 47,
-- the 30th day after the notice.
`no hearing, then I apply on the 30th day` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `s 64(5), (7) and s 90 -- what must happen after my request is passed on` 0 (LIST (EVENT `the coroner who made the decision` `respond to the Chief Coroner's request to reconsider` 3), (EVENT `the Chief Coroner` `give me written notice that a hearing will not be conducted, with the statements s 64(7)(b) requires` 17), (EVENT `I, the applicant` `apply to the Supreme Court for an order that a hearing be conducted` 47))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
#ASSERT `no hearing, then I apply on the 30th day` EQUALS TRUE
-- Not applying is never a breach: s 90 is a permission.
`no hearing, and I do not apply` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `s 64(5), (7) and s 90 -- what must happen after my request is passed on` 0 (LIST (EVENT `the coroner who made the decision` `respond to the Chief Coroner's request to reconsider` 3), (EVENT `the Chief Coroner` `give me written notice that a hearing will not be conducted, with the statements s 64(7)(b) requires` 17), (WAITUNTIL 60))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
#ASSERT `no hearing, and I do not apply` EQUALS TRUE
-- The coroner responds on day 15, one day late: breached, so not fulfilled.
`response one day late` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `s 64(5), (7) and s 90 -- what must happen after my request is passed on` 0 (LIST (EVENT `the coroner who made the decision` `respond to the Chief Coroner's request to reconsider` 15))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
#ASSERT `response one day late` EQUALS FALSE
#TRACE `s 64(5), (7) and s 90 -- what must happen after my request is passed on` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `the coroner who made the decision` DOES `respond to the Chief Coroner's request to reconsider` AT 15
-- expect: BREACH by the coroner who made the decision, "s 64(5): the coroner did not respond to the Chief Coroner's request within 14 days after receiving it"
-- The coroner responds on day 1; the Chief Coroner says nothing by day 20 (the deadline was day 15): not fulfilled.
`Chief Coroner silent` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `s 64(5), (7) and s 90 -- what must happen after my request is passed on` 0 (LIST (EVENT `the coroner who made the decision` `respond to the Chief Coroner's request to reconsider` 1), (WAITUNTIL 20))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
#ASSERT `Chief Coroner silent` EQUALS FALSE

-- The dates. Request received 20 December 2026: response due by 3 January 2027.
#ASSERT `Last day for the coroner's response question` (`on` 20 12 2026) EQUALS `on` 3 1 2027
-- Response received 25 February 2028: notice due by 10 March 2028 (leap year).
#ASSERT `Last day for the Chief Coroner's notice question` (`on` 25 2 2028) EQUALS `on` 10 3 2028
-- Notice received 2 January 2027: apply to the Supreme Court by 1 February 2027.
#ASSERT `Last day to apply to the Supreme Court question` (`on` 2 1 2027) EQUALS `on` 1 2 2027

§ `ss 68, 90 to 93 -- the Supreme Court and a fresh inquest`

#ASSERT `Can I apply to the Supreme Court for a hearing question` TRUE (`on` 2 1 2027) (`on` 1 2 2027) EQUALS `yes, under s 90: I am within 30 days after receiving the Chief Coroner's notice that there will be no hearing; I may also rely on ss 91 and 92`
#ASSERT `Can I apply to the Supreme Court for a hearing question` TRUE (`on` 2 1 2027) (`on` 2 2 2027) EQUALS `not under s 90, which allows 30 days after I received the Chief Coroner's notice; but ss 91 and 92 set no time limit, and the Supreme Court may order a hearing if it considers it in the interests of justice (s 91) or in the public interest or the interests of justice where the coroner does not intend to hold one (s 92)`
#ASSERT `Can I apply to the Supreme Court for a hearing question` FALSE (`on` 2 1 2027) (`on` 2 1 2027) EQUALS `not under s 90, which needs a Chief Coroner's notice that there will be no hearing (s 64(7)(b)(i)); but I may apply under ss 91 and 92, which set no time limit, serving a s 92 application on the Attorney-General`

#ASSERT `Can there be a fresh inquest question` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `possibly: at my request the Chief Coroner may arrange a fresh inquest, being satisfied of new facts or evidence of material significance not available before and that it is desirable in the public interest or the interests of justice (s 68); and the Supreme Court may quash the inquest and order another (s 93)`
#ASSERT `Can there be a fresh inquest question` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not from the Chief Coroner, who must not arrange a fresh inquest unless satisfied both that there are new facts or evidence of material significance not available before and that it is desirable in the public interest or the interests of justice (s 68(2)); but the Supreme Court may quash the inquest and order another if satisfied it is necessary or desirable in the public interest or the interests of justice (s 93), on an application served on the Attorney-General`
#ASSERT `Can there be a fresh inquest question` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not from the Chief Coroner, who must not arrange a fresh inquest unless satisfied both that there are new facts or evidence of material significance not available before and that it is desirable in the public interest or the interests of justice (s 68(2)); but the Supreme Court may quash the inquest and order another if satisfied it is necessary or desirable in the public interest or the interests of justice (s 93), on an application served on the Attorney-General`
#ASSERT `Can there be a fresh inquest question` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: ss 68 and 93 apply only once the inquest has been completed (held)`

§ `s 67 -- getting things back`

-- Arguments: entitled, a document, still needed, inquest complete.
#ASSERT `Can I get back what the coroner kept question` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the inquest is complete, and the coroner must take all reasonably practical steps to give it to the person the coroner reasonably believes is entitled to it (s 67(6))`
#ASSERT `Can I get back what the coroner kept question` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes, if I ask: the coroner no longer needs it, and must give it to me on my request (s 67(5))`
#ASSERT `Can I get back what the coroner kept question` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes, if I ask: the coroner no longer needs it, and must give it to me on my request (s 67(5))`
-- The deceased's diary, still needed: a certified copy.
#ASSERT `Can I get back what the coroner kept question` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not yet, but I am entitled to a certified copy of the document, and until I get one the coroner must let me inspect and copy it (s 67(2), (4))`
#ASSERT `Can I get back what the coroner kept question` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not yet: the coroner may keep it for as long as the inquest needs it (s 67(1)(b))`
#ASSERT `Can I get back what the coroner kept question` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: the coroner gives it back to the person who appears to the coroner to be entitled to it (s 67(5), (6))`

§ `ss 40, 65, 77, 83 -- duties and offences`

#ASSERT `Must I report the death question` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: I must report the death to a coroner or a police officer as soon as practicable; not doing so is an offence, maximum 50 penalty units, 6 months' imprisonment or both (s 77(1))`
#ASSERT `Must I report the death question` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 77(1) applies only to a person who knows of the death and has reasonable grounds to believe a coroner would have jurisdiction and that it has not been reported`
#ASSERT `Must I report the death question` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: s 77(1) applies only to a person who knows of the death and has reasonable grounds to believe a coroner would have jurisdiction and that it has not been reported`
#ASSERT `Must I report the death question` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: s 77(1) applies only to a person who knows of the death and has reasonable grounds to believe a coroner would have jurisdiction and that it has not been reported`

#ASSERT `May I enter the scene question` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: entering or interfering with an area the coroner has restricted is a strict liability offence, maximum 100 penalty units (s 65(2), (3)); viewing or inspecting it needs the coroner's authorisation (ss 23, 70)`
#ASSERT `May I enter the scene question` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `s 65 does not stop me: it does not apply to a police officer, a member of an emergency service or a person assisting the coroner, unless the coroner orders otherwise (s 65(4))`
#ASSERT `May I enter the scene question` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: entering or interfering with an area the coroner has restricted is a strict liability offence, maximum 100 penalty units (s 65(2), (3)); viewing or inspecting it needs the coroner's authorisation (ss 23, 70)`
#ASSERT `May I enter the scene question` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 65 does not stop me: the coroner has not restricted access to the area`

#ASSERT `What is the maximum penalty question` `breaching an order that a hearing be private, or a direction not to publish evidence (s 40(3))` EQUALS `Maximum penalty` WITH `penalty units` IS 100, `months of imprisonment` IS 12
#ASSERT `What is the maximum penalty question` `entering or interfering with an area the coroner has restricted (s 65(2))` EQUALS `Maximum penalty` WITH `penalty units` IS 100, `months of imprisonment` IS 0
#ASSERT `What is the maximum penalty question` `not reporting a reportable death (s 77(1))` EQUALS `Maximum penalty` WITH `penalty units` IS 50, `months of imprisonment` IS 6
#ASSERT `What is the maximum penalty question` `interfering with or removing a body or ashes to prevent or hinder a post-mortem or analysis (s 83)` EQUALS `Maximum penalty` WITH `penalty units` IS 200, `months of imprisonment` IS 24
